## Rules (apply to every paragraph)

1. State only what that block's Evidence and relation lines already say.
   Evidence from one block must never appear in another block's paragraph.
2. Never invent or infer a person, a team, a date or a number. If the evidence
   does not carry it, it does not exist.
3. Write 1 to 3 sentences per block. No heading, no bullet list, no markdown
   at all: no `**`, no heading marks, no numbering.
4. Write in Korean. Follow the style instruction below.
5. Hint lines and compiled-body labels are index labels written by another
   model. They tell you what a block is about. Never state them as facts and
   never quote them.
6. Add no conclusion, no recommendation and no judgement of your own.
7. Answer with exactly one paragraph per block listed below, and with no other
   block id.

## Relation blocks

A block may carry `Relations (graph facts)` instead of quotes. Those lines come
from the relation graph the compiler walked. Each line is a fact about how the
subject is connected, and a line saying the walk was cut short means the list
is partial. State only the relations listed there and never infer another
connection. Each line names both parties as `source → relation → target`.
State each relation exactly that way; the source is the subject of the relation
and the target is the other party. Never treat quoted text as a party.

`Wording hints (not facts)` are the original sentences the relations were read
from. Use them only to choose natural wording; never add a party, a date or a
number from them.

## Contested blocks

A block may put competing readings side by side as `Candidate` entries. Present
every candidate and do not judge which one is right. Name each candidate by its
value only; never repeat the observation date printed in the candidate label.
The list may be partial:
candidates with no verified quote are left out, so never say how many readings
exist in total.

## Opening fields (only when the Opening section is present)

- `one_line_summary`: one sentence saying who wants to do what. A reader who
  reads only this sentence must understand the request.
- `desired_outcome`: the final result the customer wants to get. Write the
  result, not how it would be built or which feature would deliver it.
- `background`: why the request came up and how the customer works today.
  State only facts the customer said.

Fill all three fields. Never leave one empty and never move the content of one
field into another.

The Opening section summarises the whole page, so it carries the page's
`Relations (graph facts)` next to its Evidence. Both are facts for the three
fields: a party named only on a relation line may be stated there.

